WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.216 --> 00:00:01.791
(clapping)

00:00:01.791 --> 00:00:02.969
- Good morning Believe Nation!

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:04.641
Today's message is.

00:00:04.641 --> 00:00:05.739
- Take risks.

00:00:05.739 --> 00:00:07.989
Over to you, John Lasseter.

00:00:11.158 --> 00:00:13.227
(rooster crows)

00:00:13.227 --> 00:00:15.752
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:15.752 --> 00:00:19.335
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:00:20.199 --> 00:00:21.928
- To create something

00:00:21.928 --> 00:00:24.836
that connects with
people, that moves people,

00:00:24.836 --> 00:00:29.394
is the most fulfilling and
satisfying work there is.

00:00:29.394 --> 00:00:32.864
For an artist, it is truly a privilege

00:00:32.864 --> 00:00:35.476
to do this for a living.

00:00:35.476 --> 00:00:37.707
But with that privilege
comes responsibility,

00:00:37.707 --> 00:00:41.991
and the privilege and
responsibility are one and the same,

00:00:41.991 --> 00:00:43.658
to do something new.

00:00:46.771 --> 00:00:50.337
I say that it is a responsibility

00:00:50.337 --> 00:00:54.034
because to really truly be creative,

00:00:54.034 --> 00:00:57.337
you have to be willing to take risks.

00:00:57.337 --> 00:01:00.436
You have to put yourself out there.

00:01:00.436 --> 00:01:03.466
You have to be willing to fail.

00:01:03.466 --> 00:01:06.865
Failure has such a negative
connotation in our society,

00:01:06.865 --> 00:01:08.432
and I think this is totally unfair,

00:01:08.432 --> 00:01:11.190
because what failure really is,

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:13.866
is the opportunity to learn.

00:01:13.866 --> 00:01:17.140
If we never failed, it
would never occur to us

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.520
to do things differently.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.765
You would just keep doing the same thing

00:01:21.765 --> 00:01:24.237
because it would always work.

00:01:24.237 --> 00:01:27.320
But the world changes, people change,

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:30.293
and so we have to change.

00:01:31.275 --> 00:01:33.518
We have to fail in order to succeed.

00:01:33.518 --> 00:01:34.568
- So what do you think?

00:01:34.568 --> 00:01:35.406
- I thought it was really interesting

00:01:35.406 --> 00:01:38.388
because I resonate with it so well,

00:01:38.388 --> 00:01:41.049
just having going through
tons of challenges myself.

00:01:41.049 --> 00:01:44.393
He talks about failure, and
the only way I feel you could

00:01:44.393 --> 00:01:46.813
reach failure is to take the risk.

00:01:46.813 --> 00:01:48.096
The funny thing is, when he said,

00:01:48.096 --> 00:01:49.791
people are afraid of failure,

00:01:49.791 --> 00:01:51.956
the interesting I found is
when I connect with friends

00:01:51.956 --> 00:01:53.814
and when I see people
connecting with each other,

00:01:53.814 --> 00:01:55.689
they seem to connect through
the lessons they learned

00:01:55.689 --> 00:01:56.693
from their failures.

00:01:56.693 --> 00:01:58.139
And just by the virtue of that,

00:01:58.139 --> 00:02:00.246
I think it's so important
to take that risk

00:02:00.246 --> 00:02:02.503
and embark on a journey where failure

00:02:02.503 --> 00:02:04.083
can be a wonderful step.

00:02:04.083 --> 00:02:06.827
You can create a memory
and share with other people

00:02:06.827 --> 00:02:08.176
and grow and build wisdom.

00:02:08.176 --> 00:02:10.386
- I think we have opportunities every day

00:02:10.386 --> 00:02:12.302
to push our boundaries a little bit,

00:02:12.302 --> 00:02:14.374
to say yes to something
that we are afraid of.

00:02:14.374 --> 00:02:16.396
So as an example, even
you being here right now,

00:02:16.396 --> 00:02:18.636
you know, Tuan bought
10 copies of my book,

00:02:18.636 --> 00:02:21.196
he's in town from Ottawa,
has a whole day planned,

00:02:21.196 --> 00:02:22.311
and part of it is being here,

00:02:22.311 --> 00:02:25.009
and initially it was just
come and get a book signed,

00:02:25.009 --> 00:02:26.316
and say hi and continue on.

00:02:26.316 --> 00:02:29.048
I said, hey, why don't
we make a video together?

00:02:29.048 --> 00:02:30.777
And it was a risk.

00:02:30.777 --> 00:02:31.610
- Yeah, it was a risk.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:33.122
- He was scared to do
it, but you know what,

00:02:33.122 --> 00:02:33.955
I'm going to do it.

00:02:33.955 --> 00:02:35.924
And so you have chances every single day

00:02:35.924 --> 00:02:38.077
to say yes to an opportunity

00:02:38.077 --> 00:02:40.865
that may be scary, that
may push some boundaries,

00:02:40.865 --> 00:02:43.681
that may make you, you
know, a little fearful.

00:02:43.681 --> 00:02:44.853
Hayden's going to jump.

00:02:44.853 --> 00:02:46.136
- Yay.

00:02:46.136 --> 00:02:48.560
The interesting thing here is that

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:49.681
I'm new to this world,

00:02:49.681 --> 00:02:51.840
so if you guys are kind of
thinking about something

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.826
that you're not familiar with,

00:02:53.826 --> 00:02:55.366
this is something I'm completely new to

00:02:55.366 --> 00:02:57.424
in terms of reaching out to influencers

00:02:57.424 --> 00:02:58.977
and really cool people
that have inspired me,

00:02:58.977 --> 00:03:00.841
like Evan, and all I wanted was,

00:03:00.841 --> 00:03:04.103
hey, why don't just ask for an autograph?

00:03:04.103 --> 00:03:05.448
That's all I was looking for,

00:03:05.448 --> 00:03:07.033
and who would've known
that taking that risk

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:11.087
gave me the opportunity to
be here, on the Entspresso.

00:03:11.087 --> 00:03:12.773
This is sick, like, this is crazy.

00:03:12.773 --> 00:03:14.021
It's amazing.

00:03:14.021 --> 00:03:16.329
- A lot of people say no, a
lot of people are too afraid,

00:03:16.329 --> 00:03:18.681
or a lot of people turn
down the opportunity.

00:03:18.681 --> 00:03:20.505
A lot of people don't
even bother reaching out

00:03:20.505 --> 00:03:22.069
to say hey I'm coming to town.

00:03:22.069 --> 00:03:24.555
Can I stop by for five
minutes to sign a book,

00:03:24.555 --> 00:03:25.587
tr have a book signed?

00:03:25.587 --> 00:03:26.733
A lot of people don't take that risk

00:03:26.733 --> 00:03:29.317
because they're just
afraid of being rejected,

00:03:29.317 --> 00:03:31.477
and so, kudos to you.

00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:32.310
- Thank you.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:33.143
- For coming out.

00:03:33.143 --> 00:03:34.432
Didn't he do a good job?

00:03:34.432 --> 00:03:35.430
That was a great job.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:37.200
And for you guys watching,

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.692
you know, the next time
you have that thought

00:03:38.692 --> 00:03:39.796
of something you want to do

00:03:39.796 --> 00:03:41.664
and that voice then comes to mind,

00:03:41.664 --> 00:03:44.178
says hmm, maybe I shouldn't do it.

00:03:44.178 --> 00:03:46.627
Stomp that other voice out
and just give it a shot

00:03:46.627 --> 00:03:48.141
and see how it turns out.

00:03:48.141 --> 00:03:49.716
So the question of the day today is.

00:03:49.716 --> 00:03:52.135
- What risk are you going to take in 2017?

00:03:52.135 --> 00:03:52.968
- I love it.

00:03:52.968 --> 00:03:54.255
Leave it down in the comments below

00:03:54.255 --> 00:03:55.974
and we're going to join in the discussion.

00:03:55.974 --> 00:03:57.528
So thank you guys so much for watching.

00:03:57.528 --> 00:03:59.569
We believe in you and hope you continue

00:03:59.569 --> 00:04:03.403
to believe in yourself, and
whatever your one word is,

00:04:03.403 --> 00:04:05.404
much love, and we'll see
you again tomorrow morning

00:04:05.404 --> 00:04:06.367
for another.

00:04:06.367 --> 00:04:08.615
- Shot of Entspresso.

00:04:08.615 --> 00:04:11.129
♫ I wake up every morning

00:04:11.129 --> 00:04:14.833
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:04:14.833 --> 00:04:15.947
- Guys, we're also looking at doing

00:04:15.947 --> 00:04:17.921
a top 10 on John Lasseter.

00:04:17.921 --> 00:04:19.291
Here is a sneak peek

00:04:19.291 --> 00:04:21.160
at some of the clips that are to come.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.487
I hope you enjoy.

00:04:22.487 --> 00:04:25.081
- The thing I always tell students

00:04:25.081 --> 00:04:28.081
that really want to work in animation,

00:04:29.246 --> 00:04:31.376
or work in film, for that matter,

00:04:31.376 --> 00:04:32.709
is do not forget

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:35.377
to study the basics.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:40.380
Basic drawing, even
for computer animation,

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:41.940
you should know how to draw.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:43.023
Basic design,

00:04:45.005 --> 00:04:47.451
basic fundamentals of animation,

00:04:47.451 --> 00:04:50.096
where you learn the
principles of animation.

00:04:50.096 --> 00:04:51.700
Film grammar.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:54.294
How do you stage things?

00:04:54.294 --> 00:04:58.461
You know, how they do it
in live action filmmaking.

00:04:59.501 --> 00:05:03.560
Story, writing, three-act
story structures,

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:04.977
so vital to this.

00:05:06.205 --> 00:05:08.745
You've got to learn all these basics

00:05:08.745 --> 00:05:12.812
because this is kind of
like eating vegetables.

00:05:12.812 --> 00:05:13.853
A lot of people don't want to do it

00:05:13.853 --> 00:05:15.540
and they just get on to the more

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:19.256
flashy stuff of using
all the latest software,

00:05:19.256 --> 00:05:21.655
but the thing is, is through your career,

00:05:21.655 --> 00:05:23.738
the software will change.

00:05:24.784 --> 00:05:27.330
It will always evolve and get better.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.207
What's important, and, remember this,

00:05:29.207 --> 00:05:32.713
software never makes a movie entertaining,

00:05:32.713 --> 00:05:34.321
it's what you do with the software,

00:05:34.321 --> 00:05:38.478
and what you do with the software will be,

00:05:38.478 --> 00:05:42.710
you know, you'll learn that
from the basic fundamentals.

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:46.377
You know, I rely on
these basic fundamentals

00:05:48.223 --> 00:05:50.440
of art, and design,

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:53.559
and filmmaking, and
animation, and storytelling,

00:05:53.559 --> 00:05:55.742
every single day of my career.

00:05:55.742 --> 00:05:57.535
It's something that just is a part of you.

00:05:57.535 --> 00:06:00.035
It's the foundation in which you work,

00:06:00.035 --> 00:06:03.184
and without those, you won't go anywhere.

00:06:03.184 --> 00:06:06.149
So it's really important to
get the fundamentals down.

00:06:06.149 --> 00:06:07.659
Loved animation,

00:06:07.659 --> 00:06:10.886
I knew was not just for kids.

00:06:10.886 --> 00:06:14.665
Walt Disney never made
his movies just for kids.

00:06:14.665 --> 00:06:17.214
He made them for everybody.

00:06:17.214 --> 00:06:19.631
Chuck Jones made his cartoons

00:06:20.488 --> 00:06:21.678
to show in theaters

00:06:21.678 --> 00:06:25.764
in front of Warner
Brothers movies for adults.

00:06:25.764 --> 00:06:30.531
But I felt it had to be
done for today's audience.

00:06:30.531 --> 00:06:33.114
It had to be done in a new way.

00:06:34.117 --> 00:06:36.364
I found the answer when
I was working at Disney,

00:06:36.364 --> 00:06:38.013
computer animation.

00:06:38.013 --> 00:06:38.929
I knew this was it,

00:06:38.929 --> 00:06:41.781
I knew this was the future.

00:06:41.781 --> 00:06:44.798
You see, one of the things
that I always admired

00:06:44.798 --> 00:06:48.465
about Walt Disney was
his innovative spirit.

00:06:50.181 --> 00:06:53.681
Walt had been a great advocate of the new,

00:06:55.318 --> 00:06:58.006
and many groundbreaking
developments of film technology

00:06:58.006 --> 00:07:00.338
had their origins at his studio.

00:07:00.338 --> 00:07:03.823
I threw myself into suggesting projects

00:07:03.823 --> 00:07:06.319
that would show how the computer

00:07:06.319 --> 00:07:10.887
could be used to take Disney
animation to the next level.

00:07:10.887 --> 00:07:12.387
I even did a test.

00:07:13.941 --> 00:07:16.080
I kept getting the answers.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:18.553
No, that's not how it's done.

00:07:18.553 --> 00:07:20.655
Is it cheaper, is it faster?

00:07:20.655 --> 00:07:21.488
Then, no.

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:24.553
Just do what you're told.

00:07:25.511 --> 00:07:28.688
No one was willing to take risks.

00:07:28.688 --> 00:07:31.806
But that didn't stop me, I kept trying.

00:07:31.806 --> 00:07:34.917
I just couldn't let go of the idea

00:07:34.917 --> 00:07:38.287
that computer animation was
something Disney should be doing

00:07:38.287 --> 00:07:41.591
because I knew it'd make the
studio and its films better.

00:07:41.591 --> 00:07:45.562
Then one day, the manager
of the animation department,

00:07:45.562 --> 00:07:49.865
Mr. No himself, called me into his office,

00:07:49.865 --> 00:07:50.948
and fired me.

00:07:53.546 --> 00:07:57.178
To be fired from the place of your dreams

00:07:57.178 --> 00:08:00.387
was so painful and embarrassing for me

00:08:00.387 --> 00:08:01.887
that it took decades before

00:08:01.887 --> 00:08:04.662
I could actually tell people about it.

00:08:04.662 --> 00:08:06.636
I made this kind of excuse up

00:08:06.636 --> 00:08:10.332
that I followed my dreams somewhere else.

00:08:10.332 --> 00:08:14.238
Even as I got married, it took
me a long time to tell Nancy

00:08:14.238 --> 00:08:17.329
I had actually, my wife, I
had actually gotten fired.

00:08:17.329 --> 00:08:20.850
But as fate would have
it, I met Ed Catmull

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:22.939
of the Lucasfilm Computer Division.

00:08:22.939 --> 00:08:26.224
He and his group were doing
the most innovative research

00:08:26.224 --> 00:08:28.593
in the world on computer graphics.

00:08:28.593 --> 00:08:32.586
And unlike the Disney that just fired me,

00:08:32.586 --> 00:08:37.131
Ed was so excited about
what I was trying to do,

00:08:37.131 --> 00:08:39.355
to combine classic animation principles

00:08:39.355 --> 00:08:42.012
to computer animation.

00:08:42.012 --> 00:08:43.453
I finally found someone

00:08:43.453 --> 00:08:47.070
who could see the future as I saw it.

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:50.789
He invited me to come work with him.

00:08:50.789 --> 00:08:54.796
Then in 1986, Steve
Jobs entered our lives.

00:08:54.796 --> 00:08:58.879
He was so excited about
what we were trying to do

00:08:59.963 --> 00:09:02.639
that he bought our group from Lucasfilm,

00:09:02.639 --> 00:09:06.056
and we formed a new company called Pixar.

00:09:07.033 --> 00:09:08.496
He really pushed us

00:09:08.496 --> 00:09:10.731
to do what no one else
had ever done before.

00:09:10.731 --> 00:09:12.164
The result?

00:09:12.164 --> 00:09:15.124
In 1995, we released the
first computer animated

00:09:15.124 --> 00:09:16.865
feature film ever made.

00:09:16.865 --> 00:09:18.698
We released Toy Story.

00:09:19.917 --> 00:09:22.167
(applause)

00:09:23.699 --> 00:09:25.449
To make a great film,

00:09:27.298 --> 00:09:30.404
I believe you have to do
three things really well.

00:09:30.404 --> 00:09:34.237
Tell a compelling story
that is unpredictable,

00:09:35.262 --> 00:09:38.621
keeps the audience on
the edge of their seat.

00:09:38.621 --> 00:09:41.874
You populate that story
with really appealing

00:09:41.874 --> 00:09:44.520
and memorable characters.

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:46.759
And put that story and those characters

00:09:46.759 --> 00:09:48.592
in a believable world.

00:09:50.036 --> 00:09:53.107
Believable for the story you're telling.

00:09:53.107 --> 00:09:55.661
Not realistic, we're not interested

00:09:55.661 --> 00:09:57.989
in reproducing reality.

00:09:57.989 --> 00:09:59.957
It's a believable world.

00:09:59.957 --> 00:10:02.040
So those three components

00:10:03.822 --> 00:10:05.957
are kind of the guiding

00:10:05.957 --> 00:10:07.878
structure of this exhibit.

00:10:07.878 --> 00:10:10.990
You see the development of the story

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:12.990
through our storyboards.

00:10:14.242 --> 00:10:18.997
At Pixar, we develop the
story on the written word,

00:10:18.997 --> 00:10:22.190
on the script, but just
in the very beginning,

00:10:22.190 --> 00:10:25.312
and then we go into
creating the storyboards

00:10:25.312 --> 00:10:26.395
of the story.

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:29.980
It's like a comic book
version of the story,

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:32.151
and that's where our story is developed.

00:10:32.151 --> 00:10:34.502
You see lots of that artwork,

00:10:34.502 --> 00:10:36.244
and it's very quick and very rough,

00:10:36.244 --> 00:10:37.479
but it tells a story.

00:10:37.479 --> 00:10:39.062
It's amazing.

00:10:39.062 --> 00:10:40.174
You know, when you know our films,

00:10:40.174 --> 00:10:42.507
you look up there and you go,

00:10:42.507 --> 00:10:44.394
Oh, that's, that's the movie.

00:10:44.394 --> 00:10:45.644
It's fantastic.

00:10:46.944 --> 00:10:49.787
Developing the character design

00:10:49.787 --> 00:10:53.061
goes hand in hand with
developing the story.

00:10:53.061 --> 00:10:57.228
And you see many many versions
of certain characters.

00:10:58.432 --> 00:11:01.195
We really do a tremendous
amount of experimentation

00:11:01.195 --> 00:11:04.522
in this way, so it's very interesting.

00:11:04.522 --> 00:11:07.319
And we produce three
dimensional sculptures

00:11:07.319 --> 00:11:08.462
for our characters,

00:11:08.462 --> 00:11:11.290
because we always start with
a two dimensional drawing,

00:11:11.290 --> 00:11:14.075
but our final product is
truly three dimensional

00:11:14.075 --> 00:11:16.106
within the computer.

00:11:16.106 --> 00:11:19.162
And then the worlds that we create

00:11:19.162 --> 00:11:21.025
have to be so believable,

00:11:21.025 --> 00:11:22.767
and we do a tremendous amount of research

00:11:22.767 --> 00:11:24.595
for whatever worlds we're creating,

00:11:24.595 --> 00:11:28.228
but we want you to believe
that these worlds exist

00:11:28.228 --> 00:11:29.721
on the final screen.

00:11:29.721 --> 00:11:32.757
So you will see some unbelievable art,

00:11:32.757 --> 00:11:37.337
landscape paintings,
drawings, from tiny details,

00:11:37.337 --> 00:11:39.317
because in our world,

00:11:39.317 --> 00:11:40.900
computer animation,

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:44.053
you get nothing for free.

00:11:45.661 --> 00:11:47.844
You can't go on location

00:11:47.844 --> 00:11:49.226
and shoot a computer animated film.

00:11:49.226 --> 00:11:53.133
Everything is created
by the artists at Pixar,

00:11:53.133 --> 00:11:54.352
so you'll see artwork

00:11:54.352 --> 00:11:57.269
from all of those three categories.

